---
// Where the landing page's argument went when the tools took its place.
//
// NOT a new per-tool detail page, and not new prose: this is the essay, the boundary
// diagram and the "how you can check any of this" section that used to sit inline on
// `/`, moved whole. The landing page kept the claim as one line and linked here.
//
// It is the one route this rework adds. `tools/first-load.mjs` weighs every `index.html`
// it finds, so this page joins the size budget's set of landing routes automatically —
// and `size-budget.test.ts` asserts the heaviest route is still the one it recorded,
// rather than quietly re-recording a new one.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works — and how to check it | Not Only PDF"
  description="What crosses the boundary when you open a file in Not Only PDF, what does not, and how to watch for yourself in your browser's network tab."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>How this works</h1>

    <p>
      Not Only PDF is a set of file tools that run inside your browser. Today they work on PDFs; the
      name is a promise about where that goes, not a claim about what is here yet. When you open a
      file, it is read by code running on your own machine. It is never sent anywhere, because there
      is nowhere to send it: this is a static site with no server that could receive a file.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
    THE HERO IS THE BOUNDARY, DRAWN. Not an illustration standing in for evidence — it
    names the real pieces a file passes through and the one thing that does not happen to
    it.

    IT IS MARKUP, NOT AN SVG, AND THAT WAS A CORRECTION. The first version was an inline
    <svg>, chosen because `img-src 'self'` admits no `data:` URI (ADR 0014) and a separate
    .svg would be a second request. Both true, and both beside the point: text inside an
    SVG scales with the drawing, so at 390px the whole diagram — the most important thing
    on the page — rendered at about 6px and could not be read. Screenshotting it is how
    that was found.

    As markup it reflows: the row becomes a column, the dashed boundary moves from the side
    to the top, and every word is real text at the reader's own font size, selectable and
    translatable. The <ol> is deliberate — a file really does pass through these in order —
    but the numbers are not shown, because a three-item flow does not need counting.
  */
  }
  <figure class="boundary">
    <div class="boundary__here">
      <p class="boundary__label">this computer</p>
      <ol class="boundary__steps" role="list">
        <li><b>your file</b><span>stays here</span></li>
        <li><b>Not Only PDF</b><span>WebAssembly</span></li>
        <li><b>the result</b><span>you save it</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="boundary__elsewhere">
      <p class="boundary__label">everywhere else</p>
      <p class="boundary__nothing">nothing</p>
      <p class="boundary__note">no upload, no account, no record of what you open</p>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      Nothing crosses the dashed line. The page itself is downloaded from a server, like any page
      &mdash; that happens before you choose a file, and nothing is requested afterwards.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="read">
    <h2>How you can check any of this</h2>

    <p>
      Open your browser's developer tools, go to the network tab, and use the tools. After the page
      and its engines have loaded, you will see no further requests — not while a file is open, not
      when one finishes. The same thing is asserted on every commit, in three browsers, by a test
      that reads the web server's own log rather than the browser's report of it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The code is <a href="https://github.com/TensorGreed/burrow">public</a>, and so is the
      reasoning behind it: the architecture decisions are written down, including the ones that
      turned out to be wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>What is finished and what is not</h2>

    <p>
      Every tool linked from the <a href="/">front page</a> works today. A tool is only linked once its
      page ships — there is no sign-up to wait on and nothing is announced before it works.
    </p>

    <p>
      Not Only PDF is being built in the open and is not finished. Redaction is still to come, and
      it is the one this project is most careful about. What is here has the safety work around it
      done: every operation checks its own output before handing it to you, and refuses it rather
      than give you a document that is quietly wrong.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The boundary diagram. A row of steps inside a bordered area, with a dashed edge
     separating it from what is outside. Below --bp-stack the row becomes a column and the
     dashed edge moves to the top, because a three-across diagram at 390px is six-point
     type. */
  .boundary {
    margin: var(--space-7) 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    max-width: var(--track-readout);
  }

  .boundary__here {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--edge);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .boundary__label {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .boundary__steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    /* A sequence semantically, but counting to three helps nobody read it.
       `role="list"` is on the element because WebKit strips the list role from a list whose
       `list-style` is `none` -- so the ordering semantics, which are the whole reason this
       is an <ol>, would be lost in exactly the assistive technology where they mattered. */
    list-style: none;
  }

  .boundary__steps li {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .boundary__steps b {
    display: block;
  }

  .boundary__steps span {
    display: block;
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .boundary__elsewhere {
    /* The dashed edge IS the claim. Stacked, it sits above; side by side, it sits left. */
    padding-block-start: var(--space-4);
    border-block-start: 2px dashed var(--edge);
  }

  /* The one coloured value on the page, and it is the honest one: how much of your file
     leaves this computer. */
  .boundary__nothing {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--signal);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
  }

  .boundary__note {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 22ch;
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .boundary figcaption {
    max-width: var(--track-read);
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  /* 46rem is `--bp-stack`. CSS cannot interpolate a custom property into a media query, so
     the number is repeated here; keep the two in sync. When a second page needs this
     breakpoint, make it a checked assertion rather than a comment. */
  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    .boundary {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    .boundary figcaption {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    .boundary__steps {
      grid-auto-flow: column;
      grid-auto-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .boundary__elsewhere {
      padding-block-start: 0;
      padding-inline-start: var(--space-5);
      border-block-start: none;
      border-inline-start: 2px dashed var(--edge);
    }
  }
</style>
